WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.490
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:04.420
RETO MEIER: The San
Francisco fog is clearing,

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:05.920
making room for a cloud--

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.840
Google Cloud's
fourth annual event,

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.670
where thousands of
attendees have traveled

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:11.830
from around the world
to learn, collaborate,

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:14.060
and build a cloud for everyone.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.749
Welcome to Next 2019
in San Francisco.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:25.653
Hi, everyone.

00:00:25.653 --> 00:00:27.570
Thanks for joining us
on the "Next Live Show."

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:28.400
I'm Reto Meier.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:29.550
STEPHANIE WONG: And
I'm Stephanie Wong.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.080
If you can't be here
in person this week,

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.100
we've got you covered.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:33.570
To make sure you
catch everything,

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:36.070
we have six Livestream
channels, including this one--

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:37.242
The "Next Live Show."

00:00:37.242 --> 00:00:39.450
Let's kick things off with
insights on this morning's

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:42.240
keynote from Google Cloud
leaders, Aparna Sinha, group

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:44.790
product manager, Kubernetes,
and Quentin Hardy,

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:45.690
head of editorial.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:47.140
Great to see you both.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:47.910
APARNA SINHA: Thanks
for having us.

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:48.150
QUENTIN HARDY: Good to be here.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:49.650
STEPHANIE WONG: Quentin,
we were just here together

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:52.560
nine months ago talking about
the state of cloud computing.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:53.813
And that wasn't very long ago.

00:00:53.813 --> 00:00:55.230
What have you seen
that's changed?

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:56.938
QUENTIN HARDY: That's
cloud time for you.

00:00:56.938 --> 00:00:58.110
Nine months equals a year.

00:00:58.110 --> 00:00:59.850
And two things stick out.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:01.470
One, obviously new leadership.

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.090
We have a new leader
at Google Cloud.

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:04.680
And the first keynote
is very telling.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:09.030
He's focused on customer ready,
enterprise ready, products

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:10.950
and services and
a wealth of them,

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:12.930
and customer testimonials
backing that up,

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:14.067
which is fun to see.

00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:15.400
The other thing is the products.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:16.320
A wealth of products.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.390
A year's worth of
products in nine months.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.280
I'd like to just
cite one of them--

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:23.790
the Cloud Services Platform,
which is now called Anthos.

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:28.410
Last summer when it was
launched, it was prospective.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:31.030
It was going to be an alpha
and then beta very rapidly.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:34.620
It's ready in nine months with
more features and products

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.020
and services within it
than we could imagine

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:38.220
having done this quickly.

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.230
Yay to your team!

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:46.710
And what this allows is for you
to create, deploy, update, run,

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:49.590
check, secure, all
these applications

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:53.320
on-prem in Google Cloud on all
the clouds, wherever you want.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:55.500
Just take the asset
and make it grow

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.170
and make it live
better in a system that

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:02.040
is open and secure and
aimed at customer needs.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.342
RETO MEIER: Now, Aparna,
you've been closely involved

00:02:04.342 --> 00:02:06.300
in the creation of the
Cloud Services Platform,

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.400
or Anthos now.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:11.380
Based on what TK's already
shared, it already does a lot.

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:11.880
Right?

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.710
But it's going to do even more.

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:15.177
So what will that look like?

00:02:15.177 --> 00:02:17.010
And why should that
matter to our customers?

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:17.640
Yeah.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.010
Well, know great computing
platforms don't just

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:23.790
add features or reduce cost.

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:25.440
Great computing
platforms actually

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.060
change the game entirely.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:30.940
They give customers capabilities
that they've not had before.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.000
And that's what Anthos is.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.360
Anthos can be used even if
you're not in the cloud.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:38.910
So it can be used in
your on-prem data center

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:42.210
to give you a cloud-like
environment, cloud-like agility

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:47.040
and speed, but also cloud-like
control and centralized risk

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:48.330
management.

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:52.110
And it gives both your
operators and your developers

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:53.970
a level of visibility--

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:56.430
operational visibility--
as well as ease of

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:00.528
use for the developer that is
hard to find anywhere else.

00:03:00.528 --> 00:03:02.070
And then if you do
decide that you're

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:05.040
going to use a
public cloud, Anthos

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.220
gives you what no one ask
gives you, which is choice.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:11.520
And it allows you to write
once and deploy your services

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:12.630
anywhere.

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:15.270
And that's what really
accelerates your business

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:16.180
success.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:17.640
QUENTIN HARDY:
The reason we like

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.410
open systems-- yes,
it's a better way

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:21.330
to make more secure software.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:22.710
It's better choice.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:24.100
You get more velocity.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:25.602
These are all great advantages.

00:03:25.602 --> 00:03:27.060
There's an element
of self-interest

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:28.110
here, too, though.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:29.520
By leaving it up
to the customer,

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:31.410
by giving them that
freedom and choice,

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.140
it compels us to
stay competitive.

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:35.550
We have to listen more closely.

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:37.560
We have to respond more quickly.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:39.300
And if you have
locked-in customers,

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.418
it's kind of a recipe
for mediocrity.

00:03:41.418 --> 00:03:42.210
APARNA SINHA: Yeah.

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:45.270
I think one of the things
that a lot of customers

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:47.640
like about the
cloud is that they

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:50.310
don't have to worry
about all of the things.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:52.140
They can focus on
the things that

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:54.270
are important to
them and things that

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:56.230
add value to their business.

00:03:56.230 --> 00:03:59.160
So for example,
developers and engineers

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:03.330
can focus on differentiated
capabilities that are unique.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:06.160
And the cloud takes
care of all the rest.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:07.830
But why only in the cloud?

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:09.330
You know, this is
something that you

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:11.490
want to be able to
do on-prem as well.

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:12.870
And that's what Anthos does.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:15.840
It dissolves the layers that are
underneath the differentiating

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:16.620
pieces.

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:18.760
And those are managed by cloud--

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:21.670
by Google Cloud-- or they're
managed automatically.

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:26.563
And that way your business can
focus on accelerating itself.

00:04:26.563 --> 00:04:27.480
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:29.355
It seems like it's
picking the workflows that

00:04:29.355 --> 00:04:30.840
makes sense for our customers.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:32.520
Quentin, you mentioned
before we started

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:35.190
that modernizing and migrating
was another big keynote

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:36.030
takeaway.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:37.750
How does Google Cloud's
integration with

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:40.925
and migration of enterprise
systems help our customers?

00:04:40.925 --> 00:04:43.050
QUENTIN HARDY: Exactly what
Aparna was just saying.

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:44.650
Why can't it do more?

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:47.040
And in this case, why
can't your data do more?

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.530
Why can't all that
hard work that people

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:50.920
established over decades--

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:52.920
learning their customers,
learning their market,

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:55.500
learning their products,
be taken to the cloud

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:59.250
or be empowered inside on-prem
systems and get new life,

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:03.630
get new power, find new
insights with ML and AI

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:06.960
or by employing
other cloud services,

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:09.930
just refreshing all that
and taking that legacy

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:12.473
and using that as an insight
to move into the future.

00:05:12.473 --> 00:05:14.640
STEPHANIE WONG: Speaking
of this focus on our users,

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:16.098
Quentin, what do
you think are some

00:05:16.098 --> 00:05:18.660
of the most compelling
examples of learnings

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:20.567
that come with working
with our customers?

00:05:20.567 --> 00:05:22.650
QUENTIN HARDY: You know
what's easy to do in tech?

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:24.520
Deliver a lot of complexity.

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:26.890
And some companies
love complexity

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:28.412
because it's kind
of like lock-in.

00:05:28.412 --> 00:05:30.620
If you can get somebody to
learn all that complexity,

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:31.780
they'll never learn
anything else.

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:32.650
They'll be afraid.

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:33.550
You know what's hard?

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:34.600
Simplicity.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:37.402
Simplicity on the back
end for the provider

00:05:37.402 --> 00:05:38.860
is really tough,
because you've got

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:40.793
to consider a lot
of use cases, you've

00:05:40.793 --> 00:05:42.210
got to build in a
way that's going

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:44.335
to work for a lot of
different interests and people

00:05:44.335 --> 00:05:45.460
and locations.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:47.680
That requires a lot
of careful thought.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.320
And you've got to have a
kind of empathy for what

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:51.712
the customer wants to do.

00:05:51.712 --> 00:05:53.170
What does the
customer wants to do?

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:55.390
The customer wants to do
the things it's good at.

00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:58.123
The customer-- he or
she-- wants to proceed

00:05:58.123 --> 00:06:00.040
with the things they've
learned in the market,

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:02.110
with the products
they're passionate about.

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:04.420
That might not actually
be container management.

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:06.160
That's something we're
passionate about.

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:07.490
That's something we do.

00:06:07.490 --> 00:06:10.390
And in some ways this
is economics 101.

00:06:10.390 --> 00:06:13.480
Let everybody focus on
their comparative advantage.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:15.040
We are freeing up customers.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:16.890
We are giving them
that simplicity

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:18.400
so they can get
stuff out of the way

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:21.247
and just work on the problems
to serve their customers.

00:06:21.247 --> 00:06:23.080
RETO MEIER: Aparna,
Quentin has been talking

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:24.210
a lot about business folks.

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:26.710
But what does this mean for the
operators and the developers

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:28.378
are going to be using
this technology?

00:06:28.378 --> 00:06:29.170
APARNA SINHA: Yeah.

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:32.218
Well, like I said, it's a
game-changer for the operators

00:06:32.218 --> 00:06:33.010
and the developers.

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:35.780
I would say there are
three kind of big changes.

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:38.800
Number one is the right
ones deploy anywhere.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:41.980
For developers it
means that you can

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:44.710
focus on the things
that add differentiation

00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:46.000
to your business.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:49.540
For the CIO it means,
actually, that you can really

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:51.310
concentrate your talent.

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:54.940
You don't fragment your talent
across multiple different code

00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:58.607
bases, across multiple
different types of tools.

00:06:58.607 --> 00:07:00.940
And if you think about it,
every customer I've talked to

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:04.030
always says, the talent is
the most scarce resource.

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:06.850
Particularly engineering
talent is really hard to hire.

00:07:06.850 --> 00:07:10.645
It's really hard to keep
the talent motivated.

00:07:10.645 --> 00:07:13.270
And I think the reason creators
are excited about an Anthos is,

00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:14.480
again, for the same reasons.

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:14.980
Right?

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:18.700
Because they also get access
to a multi-cloud platform.

00:07:18.700 --> 00:07:22.180
They also get access to the
benefits of something that's

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:24.820
at a level that they can
differentiate their code

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:27.700
and don't have to worry about
the underlying infrastructure

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:29.007
pieces.

00:07:29.007 --> 00:07:31.090
STEPHANIE WONG: Quentin,
Google CEO Sundar Pichai,

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:34.310
was an important part of
the day one keynote as well.

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:36.220
What did his presence
say about how

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:38.032
Google sees the cloud industry?

00:07:38.032 --> 00:07:39.490
QUENTIN HARDY:
Well, obviously, you

00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:43.240
bring out the CEO of Google,
he's putting the stamp on it

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:44.660
that this is big.

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:45.910
And you can't say that enough.

00:07:45.910 --> 00:07:48.430
And this gets kind of to an
abstract reality of where

00:07:48.430 --> 00:07:50.440
we are right now in the world.

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:55.330
There are four major players,
five, you could go to six.

00:07:55.330 --> 00:07:57.860
$5 to $10 billion in capex each.

00:07:57.860 --> 00:07:59.980
This is a very big
stakes operation

00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:03.580
because this is the
infrastructure of the future.

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:06.340
As much as you think
about airports or highways

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:09.880
or deep water ports or
telephones and telegraphs

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:13.540
in their time, this is the
infrastructure of the future

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:16.060
that we are building and our
competitors are building.

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:19.030
We're building it with a certain
spirit we've talked about today

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:21.940
we think will give the
customers the most power,

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:24.430
give individuals of any size--

00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:28.090
small company, large company,
freelancer, you name it--

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:30.850
greater energy, greater
power, greater freedom

00:08:30.850 --> 00:08:33.500
to realize what they
want to do in the world.

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:34.690
STEPHANIE WONG: Thank you
so much for joining us again

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:35.500
and sharing your insights.

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:35.980
QUENTIN HARDY: It's
great to be here.

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:36.620
APARNA SINHA: Thank you.

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:37.570
Thanks for having us.

00:08:37.570 --> 00:08:39.039
STEPHANIE WONG: We've-- only
just begun the first day here

00:08:39.039 --> 00:08:41.620
at Next '19 San Francisco, and
there's still so much coming

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:42.190
your way.

