00:10:57.415 --> 00:10:58.665
Thanks for being here, Kelsey.

00:10:58.665 --> 00:11:00.100
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
I'm happy to be here.

00:11:00.100 --> 00:11:02.142
STEPHANIE WONG: So earlier
we heard TK's keynote.

00:11:02.142 --> 00:11:03.600
What are you most excited about?

00:11:04.150 --> 00:11:06.567
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Well, given
my background in Kubernetes,

00:11:06.567 --> 00:11:09.630
I'm most excited
about Anthos, which

00:11:09.630 --> 00:11:11.670
is kind of giving
Kubernetes to more

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:12.940
people and their environments.

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:14.248
So you can run it on prem.

00:11:14.248 --> 00:11:15.540
You can run it on Google Cloud.

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:16.998
Also like the
addition of istio, so

00:11:16.998 --> 00:11:19.530
giving people the ability to
manage their networking playing

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:21.120
like they do for their compute.

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:23.910
And I think the other thing
I'm most happy about is more

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:24.870
and managed databases.

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:27.330
So now having SQL Server,
lots of our customers

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:28.620
ask for SQL Server.

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:31.205
So pairing managed databases
with something like Kubernetes

00:11:31.205 --> 00:11:32.330
is kind of a perfect match.

00:11:32.330 --> 00:11:32.790
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:34.457
And Google has a
really strong tradition

00:11:34.457 --> 00:11:37.530
of developer teams passing on
creative software innovations

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:39.990
to our emerging
developer audiences.

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:42.090
What tools can you tell
us about that are also

00:11:42.090 --> 00:11:44.370
empowering our enterprise
developers with years

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:46.150
of experience and
proprietary systems?

00:11:46.150 --> 00:11:46.230
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yeah.

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:48.420
I think for the biggest thing
is like meeting developers

00:11:48.420 --> 00:11:49.045
where they are.

00:11:49.045 --> 00:11:50.818
So kind of integrating
with their IDEs,

00:11:50.818 --> 00:11:52.735
giving them familiar
open source technologies.

00:11:52.735 --> 00:11:54.930
So a lot of developers are
running things like Dock

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:57.040
or Kubernetes on their laptop.

00:11:57.040 --> 00:11:58.230
Envoy packaged in Istio.

00:11:58.230 --> 00:12:00.480
So what we're doing is taking
all of these open source

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:02.700
projects that you may be
using on your own machines,

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:04.613
or maybe even internally,
and we're just

00:12:04.613 --> 00:12:06.030
adding a lot of
that support stuff

00:12:06.030 --> 00:12:08.830
that you would need to run it
in production in the enterprise.

00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:11.038
RETO MEIER: So we can see
that values are shifting up

00:12:11.038 --> 00:12:12.900
the stack with higher
level sets of services

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:14.460
and new abstractions.

00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:16.823
How is automation evolving
the developer experience?

00:12:16.823 --> 00:12:18.240
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
Previously we've

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:20.620
been trying to automate
a lot of bad practices.

00:12:20.620 --> 00:12:21.120
Right?

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:23.120
If you automate directly
on the virtual machine,

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:25.942
for example, now you're starting
to automate around scripts.

00:12:25.942 --> 00:12:27.900
We had a lot of configuration
management tools,

00:12:27.900 --> 00:12:29.393
but the APIs weren't there.

00:12:29.393 --> 00:12:31.560
Now as you move up the
stack, things like Kubernetes

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:34.143
give us a declarative interface
for deploying on a distributed

00:12:34.143 --> 00:12:34.890
system.

00:12:34.890 --> 00:12:37.292
So containers, batch
drives, con jobs.

00:12:37.292 --> 00:12:39.000
So now we sort of
think about automation.

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:41.195
You're finally targeting APIs.

00:12:41.195 --> 00:12:43.320
So what we were able to do
for traditional software

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:45.813
development we're now able
to do for infrastructure.

00:12:45.813 --> 00:12:47.730
So bringing things like
Istio, which gives you

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:49.770
a declarative API
for the networking,

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:51.870
things like our
serverless add-on give you

00:12:51.870 --> 00:12:53.800
a declarative API for
your service stack.

00:12:53.800 --> 00:12:55.300
So when you look
across the spectrum

00:12:55.300 --> 00:12:57.840
now for infrastructure,
you're now programming finally

00:12:57.840 --> 00:12:58.923
towards well-defined APIs.

00:12:58.923 --> 00:13:01.090
STEPHANIE WONG: So this is
sort of supporting people

00:13:01.090 --> 00:13:01.920
where they are.

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:04.680
And I want to talk about GKE
on prem and hybrid workloads.

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:06.180
What are some of
the benefits of it?

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:08.372
And what can sysadmins
kind of reap from that?

00:13:08.372 --> 00:13:09.330
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yeah.

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:11.130
I think since Kubernetes
came out, everyone was,

00:13:11.130 --> 00:13:11.910
like, I want it.

00:13:11.910 --> 00:13:12.570
Right?

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:14.970
And GKE, when you
click that button,

00:13:14.970 --> 00:13:16.470
you just get this
magical cluster,

00:13:16.470 --> 00:13:18.275
production-ready, ready to go.

00:13:18.275 --> 00:13:19.650
And the next thing
is, well, what

00:13:19.650 --> 00:13:21.267
about my existing environment?

00:13:21.267 --> 00:13:23.100
And many people have
kind of tried to do it.

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:25.940
And not everyone has the same
level of expertise or time.

00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:27.690
So the nice thing about
this is that we're

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:29.820
going to take GKE,
the same bits that we

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:33.720
used to host on GCP we're now
bringing that into your data

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:34.290
center.

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:36.623
So we're giving people clear
guidance on the lower level

00:13:36.623 --> 00:13:37.300
requirements.

00:13:37.300 --> 00:13:38.910
And then we just
layer GKE on top.

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:42.130
And then we give you the same
APIs, the same control planes.

00:13:42.130 --> 00:13:44.700
So if you're using things
like Terraform on GCP,

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:47.183
you're now able to point
that same stack in your

00:13:47.183 --> 00:13:49.350
on prem environment, get
that same workflow that you

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:50.520
love so much on GKE.

00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:51.290
RETO MEIER: Nice.

00:13:51.290 --> 00:13:53.790
Kubernetes is being
adopted and adapted

00:13:53.790 --> 00:13:55.980
to meet more and
more technical needs.

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:59.490
Tell us about how GKE Hub helps
admins with hybrid environments

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:00.690
do their jobs better.

00:14:00.690 --> 00:14:00.960
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yeah.

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:03.568
So GKE Hub. so once you kind
of get a taste of Kubernetes,

00:14:03.568 --> 00:14:05.610
the next thing is you want
Kubernetes everywhere.

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:06.110
Right?

00:14:06.110 --> 00:14:09.210
Every problem you see, you want
to rub Kubernetes on top of it.

00:14:09.210 --> 00:14:11.670
So a lot of our customers
have more than one cluster.

00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:13.020
If you think about it,
a lot of our customers,

00:14:13.020 --> 00:14:14.510
they value high availability.

00:14:14.510 --> 00:14:16.535
They'll have a cluster
per region or per zone.

00:14:16.535 --> 00:14:18.660
So once you start to have
a collection of clusters,

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:20.820
even you bring an on
prem into the mix,

00:14:20.820 --> 00:14:23.170
you need a place to aggregate
all of those things.

00:14:23.170 --> 00:14:24.930
So what GKE Hub
represents is the ability

00:14:24.930 --> 00:14:27.030
to aggregate all of your
Kubernetes clusters,

00:14:27.030 --> 00:14:28.387
even the ones on prem.

00:14:28.387 --> 00:14:30.720
And then once you have that
you have a centralized place

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:32.737
to do things like
policy management.

00:14:32.737 --> 00:14:34.320
So once you have a
Kubernetes cluster,

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:35.230
the thing you have
to think about

00:14:35.230 --> 00:14:36.940
is security across
all those clusters.

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:38.940
And one thing that's kind
of hard or error-prone

00:14:38.940 --> 00:14:40.440
is you have to try
to figure out how

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:42.430
to get your configuration
correct across all

00:14:42.430 --> 00:14:43.510
of those clusters.

00:14:43.510 --> 00:14:45.510
So the Hub is going to
be the anchor point where

00:14:45.510 --> 00:14:47.723
we build a lot of those
additional tools that

00:14:47.723 --> 00:14:49.890
allow you to manage everything
from a central place.

00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:50.677
RETO MEIER: Nice.

00:14:50.677 --> 00:14:51.510
Well, thanks Kelsey.

00:14:51.510 --> 00:14:53.340
We really appreciate
you taking the time.

00:14:53.340 --> 00:14:53.580
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Awesome.

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:54.690
Thank you.

00:14:54.690 --> 00:14:55.440
RETO MEIER: Right.

