00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:44.630
RETO MEIER: We couldn't
get enough of him,

00:18:44.630 --> 00:18:45.588
so we brought him back.

00:18:45.588 --> 00:18:48.790
Joining us once more is Kelsey
Hightower, developer advocate

00:18:48.790 --> 00:18:50.320
at Google Cloud.

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:52.900
Kelsey, talk to us about
how serverless solutions

00:18:52.900 --> 00:18:54.280
help developers.

00:18:54.280 --> 00:18:55.238
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yeah.

00:18:55.238 --> 00:18:57.790
I think serverless solutions
for people new to serverless,

00:18:57.790 --> 00:18:59.832
it's kind of like we've
learned a lot of patterns

00:18:59.832 --> 00:19:01.417
from four or five
years of Kubernetes.

00:19:01.417 --> 00:19:04.000
And all the things you need to
do to provide that higher level

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:05.792
interface that you give
all your developers

00:19:05.792 --> 00:19:08.140
or that you enjoy working
with every day, that's where

00:19:08.140 --> 00:19:09.880
serverless attempts to start.

00:19:09.880 --> 00:19:11.380
So we take some
of those patterns,

00:19:11.380 --> 00:19:14.208
and we give people the smallest
interface that they want to do.

00:19:14.208 --> 00:19:16.750
So if you think about it, if a
file gets uploaded to a Google

00:19:16.750 --> 00:19:20.050
Cloud Storage bucket, you can
just invoke your business logic

00:19:20.050 --> 00:19:21.678
and execute your code for you.

00:19:21.678 --> 00:19:23.470
STEPHANIE WONG: Now
people want serverless,

00:19:23.470 --> 00:19:25.130
but they also want containers.

00:19:25.130 --> 00:19:26.890
How is Google
enabling developers

00:19:26.890 --> 00:19:28.570
to have both the
velocity of serverless

00:19:28.570 --> 00:19:30.430
and the flexibility
of containers?

00:19:30.430 --> 00:19:30.850
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
That's the thins

00:19:30.850 --> 00:19:32.350
that I'm probably
the most proud of.

00:19:32.350 --> 00:19:35.398
So when serverless was first
introduced to most people,

00:19:35.398 --> 00:19:37.690
they always thought about it
as functions as a service.

00:19:37.690 --> 00:19:39.232
You write a single
function, an event

00:19:39.232 --> 00:19:40.907
comes in, it calls
your function.

00:19:40.907 --> 00:19:42.490
But there's a lot
of existing business

00:19:42.490 --> 00:19:43.907
logic that needs
a little bit more

00:19:43.907 --> 00:19:45.077
than just a single function.

00:19:45.077 --> 00:19:46.660
Maybe there's some
bootstrapping code.

00:19:46.660 --> 00:19:48.130
You got to set up
things like logging,

00:19:48.130 --> 00:19:49.505
but a little bit
more flexibility

00:19:49.505 --> 00:19:50.800
and your choice of runtime.

00:19:50.800 --> 00:19:53.133
So what we decided to do was
take a lot of the learnings

00:19:53.133 --> 00:19:55.720
from Kubernetes and allow
developers to deploy

00:19:55.720 --> 00:19:57.100
their own docker container.

00:19:57.100 --> 00:19:59.860
So any runtime that you want
to use, any business logic

00:19:59.860 --> 00:20:01.630
that you want, any
libraries, and we're

00:20:01.630 --> 00:20:04.850
going to execute that container
just like we do with our Cloud

00:20:04.850 --> 00:20:05.350
Function.

00:20:05.350 --> 00:20:06.850
So they scale to zero.

00:20:06.850 --> 00:20:07.683
They scale up quick.

00:20:07.683 --> 00:20:09.600
And they give you the
same serverless workflow

00:20:09.600 --> 00:20:12.310
that you're used to without
abandoning all the good things

00:20:12.310 --> 00:20:13.522
that come with containers.

00:20:13.522 --> 00:20:14.230
RETO MEIER: Nice.

00:20:14.230 --> 00:20:17.800
You've talked a little bit
about serverless and brand new.

00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:20.140
But we're changing the way
that we're approaching it.

00:20:20.140 --> 00:20:21.515
Can you tell us
a little bit more

00:20:21.515 --> 00:20:24.280
about how Google is changing
that serverless experience?

00:20:24.280 --> 00:20:27.100
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: So I think for
us is that our DNA has always

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:30.130
been around this idea of this
kind of software delivery

00:20:30.130 --> 00:20:30.630
chain.

00:20:30.630 --> 00:20:31.130
Right?

00:20:31.130 --> 00:20:33.100
So this idea that when
you build a software,

00:20:33.100 --> 00:20:34.780
we like to build it once.

00:20:34.780 --> 00:20:36.080
We sign those images.

00:20:36.080 --> 00:20:38.390
And then we decide where we
want them to be deployed.

00:20:38.390 --> 00:20:40.223
So that means giving
people a bit of choice.

00:20:40.223 --> 00:20:43.120
So typically, serverless has
been these proprietary cloud

00:20:43.120 --> 00:20:44.102
platforms.

00:20:44.102 --> 00:20:46.060
Once you're in there,
you're kind of locked in.

00:20:46.060 --> 00:20:49.150
What we decided to do is make
sure that it runs everywhere.

00:20:49.150 --> 00:20:50.650
So wherever you as
a developer want

00:20:50.650 --> 00:20:53.740
to run, just like most
Google tools and libraries,

00:20:53.740 --> 00:20:56.008
we allow our serverless stack
to run wherever you are.

00:20:56.008 --> 00:20:57.550
So I think what
we're doing different

00:20:57.550 --> 00:20:59.230
here is taking some of the
things that worked well

00:20:59.230 --> 00:21:01.127
in the Kubernetes space,
and combining them

00:21:01.127 --> 00:21:02.710
with some of the
developer experiences

00:21:02.710 --> 00:21:04.900
that we know that people are
looking for, and just combining

00:21:04.900 --> 00:21:05.420
the two.

00:21:05.420 --> 00:21:08.530
So flexibility without losing
the trade-offs and benefits

00:21:08.530 --> 00:21:09.430
of going serverless.

00:21:09.430 --> 00:21:10.390
STEPHANIE WONG: That's great.

00:21:10.390 --> 00:21:12.490
So how does serverless--
our serverless offering--

00:21:12.490 --> 00:21:13.540
help our developers?

00:21:13.540 --> 00:21:15.295
And how is it different?

00:21:16.090 --> 00:21:16.490
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
I think where it's

00:21:16.490 --> 00:21:18.490
different is that you get
to use a familiar tool

00:21:18.490 --> 00:21:20.950
chain that has been popularized
over the last couple of years.

00:21:20.950 --> 00:21:21.450
Right?

00:21:21.450 --> 00:21:23.110
So containers are
the way most people

00:21:23.110 --> 00:21:26.818
think about having full control
over their own software.

00:21:26.818 --> 00:21:28.360
Then to be able to
take that software

00:21:28.360 --> 00:21:30.610
and outsource all of
the delivery, right?

00:21:30.610 --> 00:21:33.700
So security, IM
integration, being

00:21:33.700 --> 00:21:36.280
able to connect to things
like Spanner and BigQuery,

00:21:36.280 --> 00:21:39.230
taking all of that kind of toil
away from you and just saying,

00:21:39.230 --> 00:21:41.170
hey, check in your containers.

00:21:41.170 --> 00:21:42.680
And we'll run them for you.

00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:43.570
So what we're
trying to do is kind

00:21:43.570 --> 00:21:45.320
of make it very seamless
from a developer.

00:21:45.320 --> 00:21:47.410
Once you go from
idea to deploy, we're

00:21:47.410 --> 00:21:50.110
just trying to give you
that same DCP experience

00:21:50.110 --> 00:21:51.300
at the container level.

00:21:51.300 --> 00:21:52.008
RETO MEIER: Nice.

00:21:52.008 --> 00:21:54.330
Now how do our service
offerings give developers

00:21:54.330 --> 00:21:56.175
a more open and
portable offering

00:21:56.175 --> 00:21:57.550
so that customers
can potentially

00:21:57.550 --> 00:21:58.717
move their workloads around?

00:21:58.717 --> 00:22:00.467
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: So
one thing-- the trend

00:22:00.467 --> 00:22:02.020
that has been really
prevalent in GCP

00:22:02.020 --> 00:22:05.320
is in Kubernetes we started
with docker, popular open source

00:22:05.320 --> 00:22:06.460
container runtime.

00:22:06.460 --> 00:22:08.810
And then we shipped in open
source project, Kubernetes,

00:22:08.810 --> 00:22:11.205
which is the underlying
platform for GKE.

00:22:11.205 --> 00:22:13.330
And then when we thought
about entering serverless.

00:22:13.330 --> 00:22:16.350
So we've had App Engine for
it for almost 11 years now.

00:22:16.350 --> 00:22:17.770
And we decided that--

00:22:17.770 --> 00:22:18.530
what would we do?

00:22:18.530 --> 00:22:20.680
We wanted to make App
Engine be more open.

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:23.050
So we decided to start
with the Knative project.

00:22:23.050 --> 00:22:25.060
So Knative has a lot of
serverless components

00:22:25.060 --> 00:22:26.920
you can think of as a
serverless tool kit--

00:22:26.920 --> 00:22:29.470
things like builds,
a function framework.

00:22:29.470 --> 00:22:31.960
And most of that stuff is
layered on top of Kubernetes.

00:22:31.960 --> 00:22:33.310
So what we've done is
we've taken the best

00:22:33.310 --> 00:22:35.590
parts of Kubernetes--
again, those design patterns

00:22:35.590 --> 00:22:36.550
that we talked about.

00:22:36.550 --> 00:22:38.770
How do I just run
my application?

00:22:38.770 --> 00:22:41.650
We've abstracted that out into
a high level set of libraries

00:22:41.650 --> 00:22:42.880
called Knative.

00:22:42.880 --> 00:22:45.240
And there's a hosted
version we call Cloud Run.

00:22:45.240 --> 00:22:48.832
And we also give you the ability
to run Cloud Run on top of GKE.

00:22:48.832 --> 00:22:50.290
So the benefit
there is that if you

00:22:50.290 --> 00:22:51.560
need to do some
machine learning,

00:22:51.560 --> 00:22:53.200
you can take that
serverless workload

00:22:53.200 --> 00:22:57.400
and run it on a specialized
VM with a GPU or TPU.

00:22:57.400 --> 00:23:00.060
So you're no longer restricted
to just our serverless

00:23:00.060 --> 00:23:01.240
runtime environments.

00:23:01.240 --> 00:23:04.240
You now have the whole of
GCP opened up to you even

00:23:04.240 --> 00:23:05.710
in the serverless context.

00:23:05.710 --> 00:23:06.670
STEPHANIE WONG: You
touched on this a bit.

00:23:06.670 --> 00:23:08.110
But a lot of our
customers tell us

00:23:08.110 --> 00:23:09.670
that they have
custom requirements

00:23:09.670 --> 00:23:12.640
like specific run times,
custom binaries, or workload

00:23:12.640 --> 00:23:13.600
portability.

00:23:13.600 --> 00:23:15.040
And more often
than not, they turn

00:23:15.040 --> 00:23:16.460
to containers for an answer.

00:23:16.460 --> 00:23:19.390
Can you talk about how our GKE
serverless add-on allows you

00:23:19.390 --> 00:23:21.220
to bring service to containers?

00:23:21.220 --> 00:23:21.340
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yeah.

00:23:21.340 --> 00:23:23.632
So when you think about it,
a lot of these abstractions

00:23:23.632 --> 00:23:24.400
change over time.

00:23:24.400 --> 00:23:26.470
We went from mainframe
to physical machines

00:23:26.470 --> 00:23:28.690
to virtualization
to higher level

00:23:28.690 --> 00:23:31.030
platforms like App Engine.

00:23:31.030 --> 00:23:33.400
What we now have with the
container image, this ability

00:23:33.400 --> 00:23:35.500
to build a code that you
want, use the runtime

00:23:35.500 --> 00:23:37.570
that you want, any
version that you want.

00:23:37.570 --> 00:23:39.250
And now that becomes
a portable bit.

00:23:39.250 --> 00:23:41.120
You can run this on
a virtual machine.

00:23:41.120 --> 00:23:42.390
You can run this on GKE.

00:23:42.390 --> 00:23:43.640
You can run this on Cloud Run.

00:23:43.640 --> 00:23:45.270
And can also run
it on App Engine.

00:23:45.270 --> 00:23:47.270
So I think what a developer
gets is this ability

00:23:47.270 --> 00:23:50.485
to truly write once and
deploy wherever they want.

00:23:50.485 --> 00:23:51.860
RETO MEIER: Now,
while we believe

00:23:51.860 --> 00:23:55.130
Google Cloud is a great place
to run all types of workloads,

00:23:55.130 --> 00:23:56.990
some customers,
we know that they

00:23:56.990 --> 00:23:59.460
need to run on premises
or across multiple clouds.

00:23:59.460 --> 00:23:59.960
Right?

00:23:59.960 --> 00:24:02.840
So tell us a little
bit more about Knative

00:24:02.840 --> 00:24:04.340
and its role in
giving developers

00:24:04.340 --> 00:24:07.040
the freedom to move stateless
containers pretty much

00:24:07.040 --> 00:24:07.950
anywhere.

00:24:07.950 --> 00:24:10.180
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
So our contribution

00:24:10.180 --> 00:24:12.550
to the ecosystem started
with maybe Kubernetes.

00:24:12.550 --> 00:24:13.158
Right?

00:24:13.158 --> 00:24:15.700
So Kubernetes over the four or
five years, you can see it now

00:24:15.700 --> 00:24:18.400
it's deployed and offered by
every major cloud provider.

00:24:18.400 --> 00:24:20.870
Lots of people are already
running in on-prem.

00:24:20.870 --> 00:24:22.700
So it has wide adoption.

00:24:22.700 --> 00:24:25.300
So our strategy of
layering Knative

00:24:25.300 --> 00:24:27.430
on top of Kubernetes,
this allows us

00:24:27.430 --> 00:24:29.075
to kind of reap those benefits.

00:24:29.075 --> 00:24:30.700
So the tools that
you're familiar with,

00:24:30.700 --> 00:24:32.350
we layer Knative on top.

00:24:32.350 --> 00:24:34.390
So that gives us that
portability framework

00:24:34.390 --> 00:24:34.960
underneath.

00:24:34.960 --> 00:24:36.377
So no matter where
you're running,

00:24:36.377 --> 00:24:39.570
whether it's on-prem, different
cloud provider, on GCP,

00:24:39.570 --> 00:24:41.170
you can get the
same APIs, right.

00:24:41.170 --> 00:24:43.840
So we talked earlier about
automating those APIs.

00:24:43.840 --> 00:24:45.577
That's what
Kubernetes represents.

00:24:45.577 --> 00:24:46.660
And I've always said this.

00:24:46.660 --> 00:24:48.850
Kubernetes is a platform
for building platforms.

00:24:48.850 --> 00:24:50.808
And we've chosen to build
our newest serverless

00:24:50.808 --> 00:24:52.510
platforms on top of Kubernetes.

00:24:52.510 --> 00:24:54.427
STEPHANIE WONG: Can you
talk a little bit more

00:24:54.427 --> 00:24:56.740
about how Knative provides
eventing and integrating

00:24:56.740 --> 00:24:59.150
events from Google or
third party services

00:24:59.150 --> 00:25:01.160
so you can really extend
your applications?

00:25:01.160 --> 00:25:02.770
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: So the big
thing to know about Knative--

00:25:02.770 --> 00:25:04.062
it's not just Google's Project.

00:25:04.062 --> 00:25:04.562
Right?

00:25:04.562 --> 00:25:06.130
So now we have so
many contributors--

00:25:06.130 --> 00:25:08.385
Red Hat, Cloud Foundry.

00:25:08.385 --> 00:25:09.760
So a lot of the
things that we've

00:25:09.760 --> 00:25:12.490
seen in the eventing space,
like what is an event?

00:25:12.490 --> 00:25:13.750
How should we accept it?

00:25:13.750 --> 00:25:16.420
A lot of those open protocols
like Cloud Events, all of that

00:25:16.420 --> 00:25:18.118
supported inside Knative.

00:25:18.118 --> 00:25:19.910
So Knative gives you
a couple of components

00:25:19.910 --> 00:25:21.190
we call Knative serving.

00:25:21.190 --> 00:25:24.190
Run my container, scale
it to 0, start it again

00:25:24.190 --> 00:25:25.540
when a request comes in.

00:25:25.540 --> 00:25:26.960
Then you have the
eventing piece.

00:25:26.960 --> 00:25:28.190
So there's an event.

00:25:28.190 --> 00:25:30.370
And what we wanted to do
is make an event portable.

00:25:30.370 --> 00:25:32.703
So different cloud providers
have a different collection

00:25:32.703 --> 00:25:34.495
of events, different
transports for events.

00:25:34.495 --> 00:25:35.995
So what we wanted
to do is make sure

00:25:35.995 --> 00:25:37.880
that the event framework
was very flexible.

00:25:37.880 --> 00:25:39.400
If you want to
integrate with GitHub

00:25:39.400 --> 00:25:41.920
or GCP or some other
service that you have

00:25:41.920 --> 00:25:44.535
that you defined yourself,
that's also included.

00:25:44.535 --> 00:25:45.910
So what we've done
is we've given

00:25:45.910 --> 00:25:47.110
all of these framework pieces.

00:25:47.110 --> 00:25:48.527
And what we've
done with Cloud Run

00:25:48.527 --> 00:25:50.860
is we've kind of curated
the perfect stack for you.

00:25:50.860 --> 00:25:51.610
RETO MEIER: Great.

00:25:51.610 --> 00:25:54.670
And is there a
particular way in which

00:25:54.670 --> 00:25:57.040
we're able to solve that
problem that you describe

00:25:57.040 --> 00:25:59.140
of having all of
these things living

00:25:59.140 --> 00:26:00.462
all of these different places?

00:26:00.462 --> 00:26:01.420
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yeah.

00:26:01.420 --> 00:26:04.820
So I think for us is
Kubernetes is super flexible.

00:26:04.820 --> 00:26:07.030
So what that flexibility
allows every cloud provider

00:26:07.030 --> 00:26:08.330
to do their own integrations.

00:26:08.330 --> 00:26:10.270
For example,
on-prem, we come out

00:26:10.270 --> 00:26:13.450
of the gate integrating with
things like VMware and F5.

00:26:13.450 --> 00:26:15.340
So we abstract away
some of the differences

00:26:15.340 --> 00:26:16.910
between all of these platforms.

00:26:16.910 --> 00:26:18.950
So that's what
Kubernetes' job is.

00:26:18.950 --> 00:26:20.740
Once you have that
set of abstractions,

00:26:20.740 --> 00:26:23.020
things like Knative
just sit on top

00:26:23.020 --> 00:26:25.600
and naively assume that
those abstractions are there,

00:26:25.600 --> 00:26:28.120
and that we can get on with
building those higher level

00:26:28.120 --> 00:26:28.990
platforms.

00:26:28.990 --> 00:26:31.140
So Kubernetes is a
huge part of this.

00:26:31.140 --> 00:26:32.890
And the community has
been doing this work

00:26:32.890 --> 00:26:34.210
for over four years
of abstracting them

00:26:34.210 --> 00:26:36.400
where the difference is
between these cloud providers.

00:26:36.400 --> 00:26:37.942
So whenever you
build on top of that,

00:26:37.942 --> 00:26:40.150
we just inherit all
of those benefits.

00:26:40.150 --> 00:26:41.140
STEPHANIE WONG: Well,
thank you so much, again,

00:26:41.140 --> 00:26:42.530
for joining us today, Kelsey.

00:26:42.530 --> 00:26:42.670
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Awesome.

00:26:42.670 --> 00:26:43.960
Thanks for having me.

00:26:43.960 --> 00:26:45.370
STEPHANIE WONG: If you
want to re-watch or share

00:26:45.370 --> 00:26:47.710
our conversation with Kelsey
on serverless solutions

00:26:47.710 --> 00:26:50.860
for developers,
visit g.co/nextonair.

00:26:50.860 --> 00:26:53.440
RETO MEIER: Now we turn our
attention to the next DevByte.

00:26:53.440 --> 00:26:56.980
Here's a detailed technical look
at networking design techniques

00:26:56.980 --> 00:26:58.755
for running
applications at scale.

00:26:58.755 --> 00:26:59.380
Check this out.

00:27:02.288 --> 00:27:04.330
MAHESH NARAYANAN: Like
many developers interested

00:27:04.330 --> 00:27:07.690
in microservices, you start off
dabbling with Google Kubernetes

00:27:07.690 --> 00:27:10.750
engine, our powerful cluster
manager and orchestration

00:27:10.750 --> 00:27:13.340
system for running
your docker containers.

00:27:13.340 --> 00:27:15.550
First you accept
all the defaults,

00:27:15.550 --> 00:27:18.690
and then slowly start
modifying a few parameters

00:27:18.690 --> 00:27:19.995
to support your application.

00:27:19.995 --> 00:27:21.370
BOWEI DU: As you
spend more time,

00:27:21.370 --> 00:27:23.380
your confidence increases.

00:27:23.380 --> 00:27:25.480
More traffic and
mission-critical applications

00:27:25.480 --> 00:27:27.640
move to GKE, which is great.

00:27:27.640 --> 00:27:30.490
You will be able to benefit
from GKE's advanced cluster

00:27:30.490 --> 00:27:31.690
management features.

00:27:31.690 --> 00:27:34.480
MAHESH NARAYANAN: Soon enough,
your cluster size gradually

00:27:34.480 --> 00:27:35.620
starts increasing.

00:27:35.620 --> 00:27:38.060
Things seems to be
going fine until one day

00:27:38.060 --> 00:27:40.480
when you start hitting
scalability and manageability

00:27:40.480 --> 00:27:43.330
issues that can often be
avoided by implementing

00:27:43.330 --> 00:27:46.840
some key best practices
during cluster creation.

00:27:46.840 --> 00:27:48.580
Over the next few
sections we'll be

00:27:48.580 --> 00:27:50.110
covering some of
the best practices

00:27:50.110 --> 00:27:53.230
features and designs
in GKE networking that

00:27:53.230 --> 00:27:57.010
can help you scale and
manage your GKE footprint

00:27:57.010 --> 00:27:58.250
as your business grows.

00:27:58.250 --> 00:28:01.420
BOWEI DU: First, let's talk
about VPC native clusters.

00:28:01.420 --> 00:28:05.260
As you know, Kubernetes mandates
direct pod-to-pod connectivity.

00:28:05.260 --> 00:28:08.260
But there is more than
one way to achieve this.

00:28:08.260 --> 00:28:10.360
By default, Kubernetes
uses static routes

00:28:10.360 --> 00:28:13.060
for pod networking, which
requires Kubernetes control

00:28:13.060 --> 00:28:15.730
plane to maintain these
routes to each node.

00:28:15.730 --> 00:28:18.430
This comes at a scaling cost.

00:28:18.430 --> 00:28:21.070
In GKE we give you the
option to create clusters

00:28:21.070 --> 00:28:23.080
in VPC native mode,
which provides

00:28:23.080 --> 00:28:25.270
container-native networking.

00:28:25.270 --> 00:28:28.110
VPC native node is enabled
with a single click

00:28:28.110 --> 00:28:31.060
and enables connectivity
between all the pods in your VPC

00:28:31.060 --> 00:28:33.840
without the overhead
of route scaling.

00:28:33.840 --> 00:28:35.830
The VPC control
panel automatically

00:28:35.830 --> 00:28:37.930
manages routing setup for pods.

00:28:37.930 --> 00:28:41.080
And the native integration of
container networking in the VPC

00:28:41.080 --> 00:28:44.680
fabric improves scalability and
integration between Kubernetes

00:28:44.680 --> 00:28:45.915
and other VPC features.

00:28:45.915 --> 00:28:47.290
MAHESH NARAYANAN:
Kubernetes also

00:28:47.290 --> 00:28:49.930
makes very liberal
use of IP addresses,

00:28:49.930 --> 00:28:53.050
which simplifies the system
and makes it easier to use

00:28:53.050 --> 00:28:54.040
and comprehend.

00:28:54.040 --> 00:28:56.530
But on the flip side,
this makes IP management

00:28:56.530 --> 00:28:58.840
a challenge for
Kubernetes deployment.

00:28:58.840 --> 00:29:01.180
This is exacerbated
in a hybrid deployment

00:29:01.180 --> 00:29:05.650
or where IPs are fragmented,
making IPs a scarce resource.

00:29:05.650 --> 00:29:08.880
VPC native sources are
providing flexibility

00:29:08.880 --> 00:29:10.920
in defining your cluster
skill requirements

00:29:10.920 --> 00:29:15.060
upfront for resources like
pods, nodes, and services.

00:29:15.060 --> 00:29:18.540
So instead of having 100
resources and thousands of IPs,

00:29:18.540 --> 00:29:22.440
you can now have 100
resources and 128 IPs.

00:29:22.440 --> 00:29:25.950
This allows for Kubernetes
to reduce its IP footprint,

00:29:25.950 --> 00:29:28.600
thereby improving the
overall IP utilization.

00:29:28.600 --> 00:29:31.350
BOWEI DU: VPC native clusters
also enable other enterprise

00:29:31.350 --> 00:29:32.410
grade features.

00:29:32.410 --> 00:29:35.490
For example, sharing
VPCs between projects,

00:29:35.490 --> 00:29:39.270
IM enroll separation, providing
anti-spoofing security

00:29:39.270 --> 00:29:42.900
in our SDN stack, and also
private clusters for cluster

00:29:42.900 --> 00:29:43.683
isolation.

00:29:43.683 --> 00:29:45.600
MAHESH NARAYANAN: Apart
from scaling, security

00:29:45.600 --> 00:29:47.310
is another criteria
that needs to be

00:29:47.310 --> 00:29:49.590
considered in best practice.

00:29:49.590 --> 00:29:51.870
Enterprise users
require a deployment

00:29:51.870 --> 00:29:54.180
in which your
services and networks

00:29:54.180 --> 00:29:58.080
are isolated and accessible
only within trusted boundaries.

00:29:58.080 --> 00:30:00.780
GKE provides this
through private clusters.

00:30:00.780 --> 00:30:04.110
Private clusters makes the local
node and the cluster master

00:30:04.110 --> 00:30:06.930
accessible only within
the trusted boundaries.

00:30:06.930 --> 00:30:09.270
Any unauthorized access
to your control plane

00:30:09.270 --> 00:30:10.960
is set to default deny.

00:30:10.960 --> 00:30:13.200
For your [INAUDIBLE]
use case in enterprise,

00:30:13.200 --> 00:30:16.050
private cluster also provides
a walled garden access

00:30:16.050 --> 00:30:18.530
to control plane to
private end points, which

00:30:18.530 --> 00:30:19.908
is provisioned by the customer.

00:30:19.908 --> 00:30:21.450
BOWEI DU: Another
best practice to be

00:30:21.450 --> 00:30:24.060
aware of for
applications at scale

00:30:24.060 --> 00:30:27.410
is how you expose your
services to the internet.

00:30:27.410 --> 00:30:29.160
When you need to deploy
your applications,

00:30:29.160 --> 00:30:31.380
you may start off using
a cluster-based ingress

00:30:31.380 --> 00:30:34.883
controller, such as
Nginx or HAProxy.

00:30:34.883 --> 00:30:36.550
This is really familiar
for a lot of us.

00:30:36.550 --> 00:30:39.127
It's just a standard proxy that
you would run on your laptop.

00:30:39.127 --> 00:30:40.710
You would then expose
your application

00:30:40.710 --> 00:30:43.380
to the internet using a layer
4 Kubernetes service load

00:30:43.380 --> 00:30:45.030
balancer.

00:30:45.030 --> 00:30:47.310
However, when your
application scales up,

00:30:47.310 --> 00:30:51.030
you will want to use the Google
Ingress implementation in GKE.

00:30:51.030 --> 00:30:54.450
The GKE container native
ingress is a globally scalable,

00:30:54.450 --> 00:30:56.850
low latency
distributed proxy layer

00:30:56.850 --> 00:30:58.980
that supports many of
the built-in features

00:30:58.980 --> 00:31:02.700
such as caching, authentication,
and DDoS protection.

00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:04.620
You won't want to
fiddle with how many

00:31:04.620 --> 00:31:07.080
instances of your proxies
to scale up to, or worry

00:31:07.080 --> 00:31:09.118
about how to absorb an attack.

00:31:09.118 --> 00:31:11.160
MAHESH NARAYANAN: In
addition, the Google Ingress

00:31:11.160 --> 00:31:14.250
has built-in support for
container-native load balancing

00:31:14.250 --> 00:31:16.380
using network endpoint groups.

00:31:16.380 --> 00:31:18.330
This means that
the network traffic

00:31:18.330 --> 00:31:21.150
will be balanced directly to
the container in your VPC,

00:31:21.150 --> 00:31:24.300
skipping the order of bouncing
through the Kubernetes KUBE

00:31:24.300 --> 00:31:25.470
proxy layer.

00:31:25.470 --> 00:31:27.840
You'll also be able to
easily debug your traffic

00:31:27.840 --> 00:31:30.780
as your connections no longer
need address translation

00:31:30.780 --> 00:31:32.520
to get to their destination.

00:31:32.520 --> 00:31:35.100
There are also other
subtle advantages

00:31:35.100 --> 00:31:38.230
of using Google Ingress instead
of cluster-based controllers,

00:31:38.230 --> 00:31:41.330
such as connection imbalance,
and accurate end-to-end health

00:31:41.330 --> 00:31:42.830
checks for your back ends.

00:31:42.830 --> 00:31:44.940
BOWEI DU: GKE he makes
the job of deploying

00:31:44.940 --> 00:31:48.210
a scalable Kubernetes
much less daunting,

00:31:48.210 --> 00:31:51.360
and offers many features that
allow you to future-proof

00:31:51.360 --> 00:31:52.453
your deployment.

00:31:52.453 --> 00:31:54.870
MAHESH NARAYANAN: We have only
scratched the surface here.

00:31:54.870 --> 00:31:56.328
And there are many
other techniques

00:31:56.328 --> 00:32:00.150
that helps in scalability and
manageability of your cluster.

