Take a look.

00:42:49.690 --> 00:42:53.160
ANKIT MEHTA: GCP is a set
of solutions and products

00:42:53.160 --> 00:42:57.030
that can help you solve your
toughest business challenges.

00:42:57.030 --> 00:43:00.810
We have external
paying customers

00:43:00.810 --> 00:43:06.930
that run businesses on our
platforms and at massive scale.

00:43:06.930 --> 00:43:12.570
These customers expect
developer velocity and quality.

00:43:12.570 --> 00:43:14.580
MITHRA RAJAH: We have
thousands of engineers

00:43:14.580 --> 00:43:19.080
at Google building this platform
at all levels of the stack.

00:43:19.080 --> 00:43:22.290
These engineers want a
seamless developer workflow,

00:43:22.290 --> 00:43:25.990
deterministic signals,
insights, and velocity.

00:43:25.990 --> 00:43:29.610
ANKIT MEHTA: It is GCP
engineering productivity team's

00:43:29.610 --> 00:43:34.320
role to balance these two
dimensions between developer

00:43:34.320 --> 00:43:38.450
velocity and quality.

00:43:38.450 --> 00:43:45.060
For example, we deployed
GCP's console twice a week

00:43:45.060 --> 00:43:49.400
to our users, and
with high quality.

00:43:49.400 --> 00:43:55.010
Our goal is to establish
bi-weekly high quality releases

00:43:55.010 --> 00:43:56.570
across GCP.

00:43:56.570 --> 00:43:58.970
MITHRA RAJAH: At
Google, we have created

00:43:58.970 --> 00:44:02.690
a set of guiding principles
that let us have both developer

00:44:02.690 --> 00:44:05.030
velocity and quality.

00:44:05.030 --> 00:44:08.360
We want to share these
principles and the tools that

00:44:08.360 --> 00:44:11.510
are available to you
so you can accelerate

00:44:11.510 --> 00:44:13.780
at the pace your users expect.

00:44:13.780 --> 00:44:16.010
ANKIT MEHTA: While we
at Google initially

00:44:16.010 --> 00:44:19.400
focused on test
engineering, over

00:44:19.400 --> 00:44:23.060
a decade ago we started looking
at engineering productivity

00:44:23.060 --> 00:44:24.050
holistically.

00:44:24.050 --> 00:44:27.620
This gave us the
framework to achieve

00:44:27.620 --> 00:44:32.662
the right balance between
developer velocity and quality.

00:44:32.662 --> 00:44:34.370
MITHRA RAJAH: This
meant that in addition

00:44:34.370 --> 00:44:37.790
to doing complex
qualification across systems,

00:44:37.790 --> 00:44:41.930
we started focusing on tooling
and infrastructure that enabled

00:44:41.930 --> 00:44:46.040
developers themselves to qualify
their code changes before they

00:44:46.040 --> 00:44:47.900
commit them.

00:44:47.900 --> 00:44:52.040
ANKIT MEHTA: This in
turn allows us not only

00:44:52.040 --> 00:44:56.000
to catch bugs, but
prevent them too.

00:44:56.000 --> 00:44:59.600
This changed the way
we built, qualified,

00:44:59.600 --> 00:45:02.610
and deployed our applications.

00:45:02.610 --> 00:45:06.350
And we started pushing the
boundaries of both developer

00:45:06.350 --> 00:45:09.530
velocity and quality.

00:45:09.530 --> 00:45:13.520
MITHRA RAJAH: We balance
developer velocity and quality

00:45:13.520 --> 00:45:17.240
at Google and on GCP
through Project Help--

00:45:17.240 --> 00:45:18.330
pH.

00:45:18.330 --> 00:45:22.010
We track key metrics across
developer productivity,

00:45:22.010 --> 00:45:26.700
release velocity, reliability,
and most importantly, quality.

00:45:26.700 --> 00:45:29.560
We automatically
calculate a pH level

00:45:29.560 --> 00:45:33.800
and aim to achieve a pH
level of 5 for all projects.

00:45:33.800 --> 00:45:35.390
ANKIT MEHTA: Our
guiding principles

00:45:35.390 --> 00:45:39.500
are driving test
health, avoiding

00:45:39.500 --> 00:45:43.390
performance regressions,
and high quality automated

00:45:43.390 --> 00:45:52.030
releases, all of which are
anchored in actionable metrics.

00:45:52.030 --> 00:45:55.180
Let's zoom in on two of these--

00:45:55.180 --> 00:46:00.430
driving test health and avoiding
performance regressions.

00:46:00.430 --> 00:46:06.070
For driving test health we aim
for hermetic integration tests,

00:46:06.070 --> 00:46:11.740
fast resubmits, and
elimination of test flakiness.

00:46:11.740 --> 00:46:16.270
We have built a cloud
integration testing framework

00:46:16.270 --> 00:46:22.450
internally that helps us
write test once, and run them

00:46:22.450 --> 00:46:24.850
against any environment.

00:46:24.850 --> 00:46:26.440
MITHRA RAJAH: We
enable developers

00:46:26.440 --> 00:46:29.500
to catch performance issues
early in the development

00:46:29.500 --> 00:46:32.620
process to avoid
performance regressions.

00:46:32.620 --> 00:46:35.260
We have a framework
that enables developers

00:46:35.260 --> 00:46:38.710
to create sustainable
performance automation that

00:46:38.710 --> 00:46:42.070
can gate releases on
performance criteria.

00:46:42.070 --> 00:46:47.200
And we aim to improve GCP's
quality and deployment

00:46:47.200 --> 00:46:51.180
by reducing manual toil,
increasing automation,

00:46:51.180 --> 00:46:53.590
eliminating cherry
picks, and improving

00:46:53.590 --> 00:46:56.410
the fidelity of the test
signal in our pre-production

00:46:56.410 --> 00:46:57.950
environments.

00:46:57.950 --> 00:46:59.950
ANKIT MEHTA: Let's
dive deeper into what

00:46:59.950 --> 00:47:03.970
we're doing for the
external developer and user.

00:47:03.970 --> 00:47:08.510
Google works closely with the
external Kubernetes and Istio

00:47:08.510 --> 00:47:13.130
developer communities to
enable developer productivity

00:47:13.130 --> 00:47:14.560
and open source.

00:47:14.560 --> 00:47:18.460
Kubernetes is an
open source system

00:47:18.460 --> 00:47:22.420
for automating deployment,
scaling, and management

00:47:22.420 --> 00:47:25.000
of containerized applications.

00:47:25.000 --> 00:47:28.930
While Istio is an open
source service mesh

00:47:28.930 --> 00:47:32.860
that provides traffic
management, policy enforcement,

00:47:32.860 --> 00:47:35.290
and telemetric collection.

00:47:35.290 --> 00:47:38.980
We are active contributors
to special interest

00:47:38.980 --> 00:47:42.915
group, [INAUDIBLE]
testing, and Kubernetes.

00:47:42.915 --> 00:47:44.290
MITHRA RAJAH: Some
of the tooling

00:47:44.290 --> 00:47:48.450
we helped build in the open
source community includes prow,

00:47:48.450 --> 00:47:52.630
the continuous integration
CI system used by Kubernetes

00:47:52.630 --> 00:47:55.720
and a lot of other projects
that use Kubernetes.

00:47:55.720 --> 00:47:58.540
prow is built on
top of Kubernetes.

00:47:58.540 --> 00:48:03.050
So our CI helps test
Kubernetes on Kubernetes.

00:48:03.050 --> 00:48:07.150
ANKIT MEHTA: Testgrid provides
a grid of test results,

00:48:07.150 --> 00:48:10.630
making it easy to see
patterns over time,

00:48:10.630 --> 00:48:16.060
provides fast access, and allows
interactive sorting, filtering,

00:48:16.060 --> 00:48:18.860
grouping, and more.

00:48:18.860 --> 00:48:22.990
It is highly configurable and
integrated with Cloud Build.

00:48:22.990 --> 00:48:25.870
MITHRA RAJAH: Recently we
helped build hermetic testing

00:48:25.870 --> 00:48:27.970
using Kubernetes and Docker.

00:48:27.970 --> 00:48:31.930
Kind brings up a confirmed
Kubernetes cluster locally

00:48:31.930 --> 00:48:34.330
without any external
dependencies

00:48:34.330 --> 00:48:37.660
for testing Kubernetes
changes or integration testing

00:48:37.660 --> 00:48:40.090
Kubernetes applications.

00:48:40.090 --> 00:48:42.880
Kubernetes was designed
with portability

00:48:42.880 --> 00:48:44.950
and interoperability in mind.

00:48:44.950 --> 00:48:48.850
And providers want to enable
application portability.

00:48:48.850 --> 00:48:51.760
Google, alongside
other cloud providers,

00:48:51.760 --> 00:48:54.790
helps out with the Cloud
Native Computing Foundations

00:48:54.790 --> 00:48:58.690
conformance program, which
ensures certified Kubernetes

00:48:58.690 --> 00:49:02.260
products deliver
consistency and portability.

00:49:02.260 --> 00:49:04.090
ANKIT MEHTA: The
outside world now

00:49:04.090 --> 00:49:10.180
uses VMs, Kubernetes containers,
scalable storage, GitHub,

00:49:10.180 --> 00:49:11.470
and so on.

00:49:11.470 --> 00:49:15.120
Google's software is stored
in a single shared repository.

00:49:15.120 --> 00:49:18.760
The developer landscape
is rapidly changing.

00:49:18.760 --> 00:49:22.570
And we are evolving our
tooling and incorporating

00:49:22.570 --> 00:49:26.410
external tooling to adapt
to this changing landscape.

00:49:26.410 --> 00:49:29.440
Summarizing, we
believe that if you

00:49:29.440 --> 00:49:33.600
prioritize driving test
health, three performance

00:49:33.600 --> 00:49:39.160
as a first class citizen,
and have automated CI/CD that

00:49:39.160 --> 00:49:44.095
enables high quality releases,
and measure everything--

00:49:44.095 --> 00:49:45.970
MITHRA RAJAH: You can
have the same developer

00:49:45.970 --> 00:49:49.033
velocity and quality as we do.

