00:55:19.292 --> 00:55:20.250
So let's jump right in.

00:55:20.250 --> 00:55:20.710
STEPHANIE WONG: Great.

00:55:20.710 --> 00:55:21.710
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Cool.

00:55:21.710 --> 00:55:23.880
So I have on the left-hand
side my terminals

00:55:23.880 --> 00:55:26.710
which we are going to use
to do some deployments.

00:55:26.710 --> 00:55:31.020
And then on the right here
I have a Visual Studio Code.

00:55:31.020 --> 00:55:35.830
So we're going to start off
by creating a new application.

00:55:35.830 --> 00:55:38.730
Just create a hello
world, no GS application.

00:55:38.730 --> 00:55:40.800
Nothing fancy.

00:55:40.800 --> 00:55:43.560
So what's happening
here is we should

00:55:43.560 --> 00:55:47.110
be able to see our
application created.

00:55:50.750 --> 00:55:54.530
Once I refresh it is
showing up my application.

00:55:54.530 --> 00:55:56.720
I have the source files.

00:55:56.720 --> 00:55:59.570
The front end and the back
end are the different services

00:55:59.570 --> 00:56:01.130
that are in this application.

00:56:01.130 --> 00:56:05.270
In the front end, we
have the 8080 port that's

00:56:05.270 --> 00:56:06.440
listening to the front end.

00:56:06.440 --> 00:56:08.990
It's talking to the back end
on back end service address.

00:56:08.990 --> 00:56:12.350
And in the back end
we have the index file

00:56:12.350 --> 00:56:15.740
that's also talking to 8080.

00:56:15.740 --> 00:56:18.530
So we're going to change
that to 8081 so that we

00:56:18.530 --> 00:56:20.030
don't have a port conflict.

00:56:20.030 --> 00:56:25.290
And then at this point, if we
go and start these services,

00:56:25.290 --> 00:56:28.190
I'm gonna just check where
we are in our terminal.

00:56:28.190 --> 00:56:30.870
We're at the right place
in the front end folder.

00:56:30.870 --> 00:56:34.260
And we want to do the same
for our back end as well.

00:56:34.260 --> 00:56:38.600
So I'm gonna go into
the right folder, which

00:56:38.600 --> 00:56:41.890
is the hello world
no GS application 2.

00:56:41.890 --> 00:56:45.390
And then so we are now in back
ends and front end folders.

00:56:45.390 --> 00:56:47.510
Now if we run NPM
install, that's

00:56:47.510 --> 00:56:49.850
going to get us all
the dependencies

00:56:49.850 --> 00:56:55.700
that it needs to run both
our front end and back end

00:56:55.700 --> 00:56:56.330
services.

00:56:56.330 --> 00:57:00.240
And then once that is done, we
need to start these services.

00:57:00.240 --> 00:57:04.550
So our back end is
running on 8081.

00:57:04.550 --> 00:57:06.320
And our front end
is running on 8080.

00:57:06.320 --> 00:57:09.350
So we're ready to
test our application.

00:57:09.350 --> 00:57:11.630
Our back end is on 8081.

00:57:11.630 --> 00:57:15.107
And our front end is on 8080,
talking to our back end.

00:57:15.107 --> 00:57:17.690
So we have an application that's
running through Visual Studio

00:57:17.690 --> 00:57:19.370
Code.

00:57:19.370 --> 00:57:22.580
Now at this point, if I wanted
to deploy this application

00:57:22.580 --> 00:57:28.010
into GKE, I would first
need a cluster to create.

00:57:28.010 --> 00:57:30.230
And then once the first
cluster is created,

00:57:30.230 --> 00:57:32.280
then I can start deploying
this application.

00:57:32.280 --> 00:57:35.810
So let's see how we can create
a cluster from within Visual

00:57:35.810 --> 00:57:36.890
Studio Code.

00:57:36.890 --> 00:57:41.080
I have the extension
for Visual Studio

00:57:41.080 --> 00:57:43.820
Code called GKE extension.

00:57:43.820 --> 00:57:46.170
And once you download
that extension,

00:57:46.170 --> 00:57:47.780
you'll start to
see your projects

00:57:47.780 --> 00:57:51.950
and your clusters in
your ID environment.

00:57:51.950 --> 00:57:55.880
I'm going to just use my current
one of the current projects

00:57:55.880 --> 00:58:00.450
and create a cluster
in the current project.

00:58:00.450 --> 00:58:06.170
So my current
project is KQ Demo.

00:58:06.170 --> 00:58:09.140
And so we'll set
that as a project ID.

00:58:09.140 --> 00:58:12.560
And then it gives me an
option to select the zones.

00:58:12.560 --> 00:58:17.100
And then here we're just going
to say Test as a cluster name.

00:58:17.100 --> 00:58:19.520
And then there's
the number of nodes

00:58:19.520 --> 00:58:21.600
that you want to create
that you can change.

00:58:21.600 --> 00:58:23.420
And then also the machine types.

00:58:23.420 --> 00:58:26.930
We're just going to go with
the standard and click Create.

00:58:26.930 --> 00:58:29.960
Once I do that, it takes
maybe three or four minutes

00:58:29.960 --> 00:58:31.590
to actually create the cluster.

00:58:31.590 --> 00:58:33.170
It's spinning up
all these instances

00:58:33.170 --> 00:58:35.480
in Google Cloud for you.

00:58:35.480 --> 00:58:38.060
And if I refresh
this, you should

00:58:38.060 --> 00:58:41.540
be able to see that it's
starting to bring up the test

00:58:41.540 --> 00:58:42.530
cluster right here.

00:58:42.530 --> 00:58:44.540
It's orange, because
it's not set up yet.

00:58:44.540 --> 00:58:47.780
So give it a few minutes,
and it starts to set up.

00:58:47.780 --> 00:58:51.230
At this point we're going to
just use for the demo purposes

00:58:51.230 --> 00:58:53.360
a cluster that I
already have created.

00:58:53.360 --> 00:58:57.330
And we'll go through the
demo using that cluster.

00:58:57.330 --> 00:59:01.400
Now, once I have the
cluster, I'm ready to deploy.

00:59:01.400 --> 00:59:05.738
And I need a skaffold yaml to
actually deploy in the cluster.

00:59:05.738 --> 00:59:06.530
STEPHANIE WONG: OK.

00:59:06.530 --> 00:59:08.113
And for those who
might not have heard

00:59:08.113 --> 00:59:09.298
of skaffold, what is that?

00:59:09.298 --> 00:59:11.090
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So
skaffold is basically

00:59:11.090 --> 00:59:13.520
a command line tool
that lets you define

00:59:13.520 --> 00:59:15.640
your Kubernetes manifest.

00:59:15.640 --> 00:59:18.860
It also defines the
container registries

00:59:18.860 --> 00:59:20.500
for the back end
and the front end,

00:59:20.500 --> 00:59:22.440
and especially for
our application here.

00:59:22.440 --> 00:59:25.130
So if you have a large
number of services

00:59:25.130 --> 00:59:26.630
that are running
on application, you

00:59:26.630 --> 00:59:29.260
would define your Container
Registry addresses in there.

00:59:29.260 --> 00:59:33.710
It would pick up
from those addresses

00:59:33.710 --> 00:59:35.673
and deploy your service in GKE.

00:59:35.673 --> 00:59:36.590
STEPHANIE WONG: Great.

00:59:36.590 --> 00:59:37.160
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:59:37.160 --> 00:59:38.880
So let's see an example of that.

00:59:38.880 --> 00:59:43.400
So I'm just first going
to go through deployment.

00:59:43.400 --> 00:59:45.510
I think I forgot
to set the cluster.

00:59:45.510 --> 00:59:47.390
So let's first set this cluster.

00:59:47.390 --> 00:59:49.670
Once it is set, then
we're going to deploy.

00:59:54.770 --> 00:59:58.790
Take the deploy and do that.

00:59:58.790 --> 01:00:00.200
Starting to deploy.

01:00:00.200 --> 01:00:03.100
While it's deploying, let's
look at our skaffold yaml.

01:00:03.100 --> 01:00:04.850
This is the yaml I was
talking about where

01:00:04.850 --> 01:00:07.040
you have the back
end and the front end

01:00:07.040 --> 01:00:10.520
and the GCR.io addresses,
which is the Container Registry

01:00:10.520 --> 01:00:15.200
address, and then the
manifest for your Kubernetes.

01:00:15.200 --> 01:00:20.610
And it seems like we have
our application deployed.

01:00:20.610 --> 01:00:23.220
So let's see the IP address.

01:00:23.220 --> 01:00:25.790
That's the IP address
of our application.

01:00:25.790 --> 01:00:29.400
It's the one that
we just created.

01:00:29.400 --> 01:00:37.310
And we see that the application
is working on port 4,000.

01:00:37.310 --> 01:00:40.100
So our front end is
talking to our back end.

01:00:40.100 --> 01:00:43.160
Both of them now deployed
on a GKE cluster.

01:00:43.160 --> 01:00:45.930
So we did all of
that from our IDI.

01:00:45.930 --> 01:00:48.265
And yeah.

01:00:48.265 --> 01:00:49.640
STEPHANIE WONG:
That's fantastic.

01:00:49.640 --> 01:00:51.432
I guess the next step
that I would think of

01:00:51.432 --> 01:00:53.360
is making changes to,
let's say, our back end

01:00:53.360 --> 01:00:54.830
without affecting our front end.

01:00:54.830 --> 01:00:56.955
So for example, what if I
want to test to make sure

01:00:56.955 --> 01:00:59.180
that my change doesn't break
anything without needing

01:00:59.180 --> 01:01:00.673
to redeploy and rebuild?

01:01:00.673 --> 01:01:02.340
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
That's a great point,

01:01:02.340 --> 01:01:04.700
because in a
development lifecycle

01:01:04.700 --> 01:01:07.310
that's the biggest hurdle.

01:01:07.310 --> 01:01:09.530
You test, you develop, you test.

01:01:09.530 --> 01:01:11.300
And then you want
to deploy that.

01:01:11.300 --> 01:01:13.640
But the test stage is
a very critical stage.

01:01:13.640 --> 01:01:16.280
So you would use something
called TelePresence for that.

01:01:16.280 --> 01:01:18.320
And TelePresence is,
again, a command line tool

01:01:18.320 --> 01:01:20.618
that helps you create a proxy.

01:01:20.618 --> 01:01:22.910
So in this case in this
example, what we're going to do

01:01:22.910 --> 01:01:27.200
is create a proxy and create
our end run, our back end,

01:01:27.200 --> 01:01:28.710
on local machine.

01:01:28.710 --> 01:01:30.410
And then our front
end would continue

01:01:30.410 --> 01:01:32.970
to run on our GKE
cluster in production.

01:01:32.970 --> 01:01:35.300
So we're able to test the
changes to our back end.

01:01:35.300 --> 01:01:36.628
But our front end stays intact.

01:01:36.628 --> 01:01:37.420
STEPHANIE WONG: OK.

01:01:37.420 --> 01:01:38.270
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
So let's see that.

01:01:38.270 --> 01:01:38.990
Yeah.

01:01:38.990 --> 01:01:42.740
So in this case here, so when
you come out of this, and let's

01:01:42.740 --> 01:01:44.960
see where we are.

01:01:44.960 --> 01:01:49.490
I want to go into
the application

01:01:49.490 --> 01:01:51.400
that we just deployed,
which is this.

01:01:51.400 --> 01:01:53.360
And let's make a
little bit of a change

01:01:53.360 --> 01:01:58.130
here in our front
end application

01:01:58.130 --> 01:02:03.110
so that we can use
a little bit of--

01:02:03.110 --> 01:02:06.490
I just called it Version
2 for our back end.

01:02:06.490 --> 01:02:09.200
And at this point, if I run
my TelePresence command,

01:02:09.200 --> 01:02:13.070
which is nothing but--
it creates a proxy,

01:02:13.070 --> 01:02:16.280
and it's going to
run it on port.

01:02:20.130 --> 01:02:20.810
So here it is.

01:02:20.810 --> 01:02:22.610
It's forwarding the ports now.

01:02:22.610 --> 01:02:27.810
And if we go test our back
end on our local machine,

01:02:27.810 --> 01:02:29.570
that's going to version 2.

01:02:29.570 --> 01:02:35.660
And it should work the same
way from our front end as well.

01:02:35.660 --> 01:02:38.117
So that's pretty
much how it works.

01:02:38.117 --> 01:02:39.450
STEPHANIE WONG: Very impressive.

01:02:39.450 --> 01:02:41.360
So we were not only
able to see you

01:02:41.360 --> 01:02:45.230
being able to redeploy using
your local IDE to GKE, using

01:02:45.230 --> 01:02:47.240
cloud code and skaffold,
but you were also

01:02:47.240 --> 01:02:50.048
able to make changes locally
to a single service, test it,

01:02:50.048 --> 01:02:52.340
and then, again, redeploy it
to GKE once we were using.

01:02:52.340 --> 01:02:53.160
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Pretty powerful.

01:02:53.160 --> 01:02:53.460
Yeah.

01:02:53.460 --> 01:02:53.630
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

01:02:53.630 --> 01:02:54.440
TelePresence.

01:02:54.440 --> 01:02:54.650
OK.

